From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Thu, 12 May 1994 11:43:23 -0400
Subject: [#94-5-174] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

 pgf@srl01.cacs.usl.edu ("Phil G. Fraering") writes in X-Message-Number: #94-5-168:
>Anyway, Nick Szabo and I have speculated that they're probably using
>GP-like algorithms now, and modelling the economy in a partial
>differential equation of (picking a number out of thin air) 150
>separate variables. Noone understands this. The President (and the
>rest of the government) has raised taxes and spending, which according
>to him should make things fine, and can't understand why anyone would
>think there's trouble ahead...

 There is a nearly universal agreement among good investors that systems
with a large number of variables work poorly because they overfit (i.e.
learn the noise in the historical data rather than concentrating on real
patterns). For instance, William Eckhardt is quoted in Jack Schwager's
_The New Market Wizards_ as saying 7 or 8 degrees of freedom is probably
too many. I am using a system that is more complex than that, but I am
working hard at reducing the problems associated with that by phasing
out my use of neural nets and putting more reliance on a few simple rules
that reflect my understanding of how the market works. (I reported in my
masters thesis, available by ftp from wilma.cs.brown.edu, file
pub/techreports/93/cs93-36.ps.Z, that I had gotten neural nets to do a
good job of predicting the stock market, but after I started trading on
that system I discovered a bug in one of my data files which caused the
reported results to be wildly inflated. The neurals nets weren't doing
quite as well as a more direct use of the inputs).

 rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu  (Ray) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-5-170:
>broker via phone was cited as being "good enough." I disagreed.
>Now here's an example. When human brokers are replaced with
>lightning fast genetic programs, opportunities and lost and won in
>milliseconds. Nowadays, you have a few hours after hearing bad news
>to do damage control with your portfolio. In the future of
>automated trading, you may only have seconds.

 Hours? Not likely. Even if there are lots of investors who haven't got
the news during that time period, a handfull of pros who got the news
quickly can knock the price most of the way down almost instantly by
competing against each to sell the stock to the suckers. (It still pays,
on average, to sell a few hours after the bad news because there is a
better than random chance that there will be more bad news in the coming
months, but once you have delayed one hour speed is of limited importance).
As an example of the speed of floor traders, Joe Ritchie is quoted in _The
New Market Wizards_ as saying that he has made money by getting good phone
clerks to relay prices used for arbitrage with a transmission time of
2 seconds vs 3-10 seconds for his competitors. (On the other hand, I
recently did some programming on a system used by a large pension fund
to integrate their electronic trading, and was surprised by the fact that
even with the improvements we made for them, their reaction times were
minutes as best).
