From: tburns@mason1.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
Date: Thu, 12 May 1994 13:04:03 -0400
Subject: [#94-5-175] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

At  2:35 AM 5/12/94 +0000, Ray wrote:
>   Despite the "magical" properties of GA/GP, if the efficient
>market hypothesis is true, they are good for nothing.

Well, yes. The whole idea of these systems is that it is possible to
consistently make a profit exceeding the market average by a statistically
significant amount. You don't need fancy computers to throw darts at the
WSJ.

>The market may not be that efficient now, but imagine what will
>happen when everyone has genetic programming brokers. The bounty will
>go to those with the most computing resources and the fastest and
>most complete information. (e.g. the elite)

This assumes that the GP's will reach some equilibrium where they agree on
the basic model of the economy, and compete only on ability to exploit that
model. While some significant convergence wouldn't surprise me, I would
expect the competition to always include some diferences in the basic
model, in which case there might be some other factors influencing the
outcomes. There would still be room for them to disagree, and for the
underdog to still come out ahead.

And there's always dumb luck!

This argument parallels Lange's exposition of technological market
socialism - use computers to replace the market. Basically, if this
computer consistently predicts what the market does, you don't need the
market anymore. Hayek would say that this discounts the importance of
local, inarticulate, tacit knowledge. Hm?

Dave            tburns@mason1.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)



